## Seed Identity Constraints (Auto-Derived)
Each reasoning workflow is a distinct reasoning strategy. Its aggregation method and structural
pattern (single-model vs multi-model) define its identity. Changing these would make the workflow
a duplicate of an existing sibling seed — which already has its own benchmark wrapper and tuning
track. Tune within the identity, not across it.

| Workflow | Aggregation | Pattern |
|----------|-------------|---------|
{{- range .SeedIdentities}}
| {{.ID}} | {{.AggregationMethod}} | {{.Pattern}} |
{{- end}}

FROZEN (never change for any workflow):
- **Aggregation method** — each method is already covered by a dedicated sibling seed
- **Structural pattern** — do not convert single-model to multi-model or vice versa
- **Node topology** — do not add/remove agent nodes (changes the ensemble size and strategy semantics)

If your analysis concludes the current aggregation method is fundamentally unsuited for the
model mix (e.g., majority vote loses correct minority answers), the right response is to
document this finding and recommend model swaps or skip tuning — NOT to switch aggregation
methods. The sibling seed with the better-suited method already exists and will be tuned
in its own benchloop session.
{{end}}
## What You Must NOT Do
- Never edit seed files (`pkg/seeds/data/*.json`). Seeds are templates loaded at server start.
  Editing them has no effect until the server restarts. Always use conctl workflows export/update.
- Do not change workflow IDs (the id field must remain the same)
- Do not break the benchmark wrapper output contract structure (input -> child_workflow -> agent-contract -> result)
- Do not change node types (do not convert an "agent" to a "prompt", etc.)
- Do not change the aggregation method (see Seed Identity Constraints above)

## Concurrency Limits
- Default concurrency: 100. You may increase up to 150 if needed.
- Hard ceiling: 150. The backend runs WORKER_COUNT=300 with MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKFLOWS=150.
  Each benchmark item spawns a parent+child workflow pair, so concurrency above 150 can
  exhaust the worker pool and cause deadlocks.
- If you believe higher concurrency is needed, do NOT increase it yourself - instead,
  note it in your decision reasoning and let the operator restart the backend.
